    "long_instructions": "## Flowcharting a For Loop\r\n\r\nExplore the model **for loop** flowchart. Then create your own **for loop** flowchart to model something that is personally relevant to you. \r\n\r\nSave your flowchart to your backpack when finished.\r\n\r\nMake sure to label your start, stop, and step values clearly. \r\n\r\nChoose from the examples below or come up with your own:\r\n- Playing a video game\r\n- Baking cookies\r\n- Practicing a free throw\r\n- Going to class \r\n\r\n:::details [What are start, stop, and step values?]\r\nWe use start, stop, and step values to control how a loop counts. Think of them like steps on a staircase. The **start value** tells you which step to start on, the **stop value** tells you which step to end on, and the **step** tells you how many stairs to take at a time.\r\n:::",
    "exemplar_sources": {
      "source": {
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "l7bMkdn8siD3BuKhq3-Er",
            "type": "shape",
